      "naturalness": "“Absolutely—two minutes, eyes open” directly and comfortably acknowledges the correction.",
      "useful_understanding": "“Notice its colors and shapes, while feeling your feet on the floor” concretely explains the proposed pause before the user decides.",
      "grounding": "“It could be a simple, eyes-open pause” presents a possible exercise without inventing access or guaranteeing benefit.",
      "recommendation_fit": "“Two minutes, eyes open” meets both constraints; “no need to push them away” avoids demanding suppression of work thoughts.",
      "user_control": "“It could be” keeps participation optional, and the assistant explains rather than starts the exercise."
    },
    "B": {
      "naturalness": "“You’d sit comfortably with your eyes open and notice something simple” is clear, conversational instruction.",
      "useful_understanding": "“Like the chair supporting you or a sound nearby” gives usable examples of what participation involves.",
      "grounding": "“Gently return to that sensation” and “no need to change your breathing” match the supplied resource instructions without claiming efficacy.",
      "recommendation_fit": "“A two-minute quiet pause with your eyes open” fits the requested duration and participation constraints.",
      "user_control": "“You can stop whenever you like” explicitly preserves control; the follow-up provides information without initiating participation."
    }
  },
  "preference": "tie",
  "uncertainty": "low",
  "rationale": "Both accept the shorter duration and eyes-open participation, explain the activity before a decision, and avoid pressure or guaranteed benefit. B explicitly permits stopping; A explicitly permits work thoughts without suppression. Neither difference establishes a decisive usefulness advantage. The selected resource ID itself receives no credit. Conversational evidence is clear, but actual delivery, timer operation, and system-level safety or privacy remain unverified.",
